enchilada: display: publish scanout-pool surfaces, trace swaps, idempotent MDP restore

Boot 26 (sweetzc=1 sweetfb_fit=1, software rendering): both pool surfaces were made ("in the
scanout pool at 0x9dd68000 / 0x9eab4000") but logged "create id 0": 0251's pool branch set
s->memory and so skipped the IOBufferMemoryDescriptor branch, which is where a surface gets
published. Every swap then named surface 0, which the copy path skips (copy avg 0-1 us) and
zcFlip treated as "nothing to show" without a word, so the screen froze/black; IOSurface.framework
also takes all id-0 surfaces for one, a likely source of the panic at reboot. Pool surfaces are now
published like the rest (table full: the create fails and free() returns the slot); the copy path
needs nothing else (kernelAddress() gives the copyback alias, getLength() the allocSize). Every
swap zcFlip does not scan in place is counted with its reason in the 10 s line, each reason is
logged once (also "no enabled layer names a surface"), and the first 8 swaps get one trace line
each (swap id, layer mask and surfaces, pool or not, path taken). The MDP restore only runs while
a flip has moved the pipe away from ABL's framebuffer (set before the first write, cleared once a
restore is taken by a frame), so display-off and panics with nothing to undo touch no register;
it has no locks, sleeping or logging, and the panic hook is taken by one CPU (atomic exchange).
"Taken by a frame" never used the TE/frame counters (boot 25: PP0 tear check off, internal vsync
counter on, autorefresh every 1, 0 TE and 0 INTF1 frames counted): it waits for the CTL_FLUSH bits
to clear, which a frame does whatever triggers it.

